-- ─── Lesson 1 · Foundations ───
('longevity', 'healthspan-vs-lifespan',
 'Healthspan vs Lifespan: The Real Goal',
 'Why "living longer" is the wrong target — and "being capable at 80" is the right one',
 'Average lifespan rose for a century while the years spent sick rose with it. The goal of this course is not more years — it is compressing decline into the smallest possible window at the very end.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['Reframes longevity from fear of death to training for capability','Introduces the marginal decade — the most useful planning tool in this course','Sets the honest frame every later lesson builds on'],
 E'Ask people if they want to live to 100 and most hesitate — because they picture the wrong thing: a decade of frailty, dependence and waiting rooms. That picture is not paranoia. Modern medicine got extremely good at keeping you *alive* with chronic disease, and mediocre at keeping you *capable* without it.\n\nSo this course does not chase lifespan. It chases **healthspan** — the years you live strong, mobile, sharp and independent.\n\n**Compression of morbidity**\nIn 1980, Stanford physician James Fries proposed the idea this entire category rests on: the goal is to *compress morbidity* — squeeze the sick, declining phase of life into the shortest possible window right before death. Two people can both die at 88. One spends their last 20 years managing decline; the other hikes at 80 and fades over months, not decades. Same lifespan, radically different lives.\n\n**The marginal decade**\nHere is the planning tool: your **marginal decade** is the last decade of your life — and whatever you can do in it, you can only do if you trained for it. Capability declines on a slope: VO2max falls roughly 10% per decade after 30 (faster after 60 if untrained), and muscle mass follows a similar slide. You cannot start the climb in the marginal decade. You can only arrive there with reserves.\n\nSo work backwards. Want to carry your own luggage at 80? Lift a grandchild at 75? Hike a fell at 85? Each of those has a strength and VO2max price — and because decline compounds, the version of you at 40 or 50 must be *far* stronger and fitter than the target, not slightly.\n\n**Why athletes have an edge — and a trap**\nIf you train, you are already building the reserve. The trap is training only for this summer: chasing a number on the bar or the scale while ignoring the capacities that decide your last 20 years. The next nine lessons rank those capacities by the strength of the mortality evidence — no wellness folklore, no supplement hype, hazard ratios first.\n\n**Our honesty contract**\nLongevity is the most hype-polluted corner of health. Every lesson here carries an evidence chip: *strong* means repeated large human studies or meta-analyses; *promising* means good but incomplete evidence; *speculative* means mechanism without outcomes. When the evidence does not support a popular claim, we say so by name.\n\nYou are not training to avoid death. You are training so the last decade of your life still belongs to you.',
 'Every protocol in this app — check-ins, streaks, training — compounds over decades. This lesson defines what it is compounding toward: not more years, but more capable years, planned backwards from your marginal decade.',
 ARRAY['Healthspan (capable years) is the target, not lifespan (total years).','Compression of morbidity: squeeze decline into the smallest window at the end (Fries, 1980).','Plan backwards from your marginal decade — the reserves you need at 80 are built decades earlier.'],
 ARRAY['Write down three physical things you want to still do in your 80s.','For each, name the capacity it needs (strength, endurance, balance) — that is your training "why".'],
 '[{"author":"Fries, J.F.","title":"Aging, natural death, and the compression of morbidity (NEJM)","year":1980},{"author":"Attia, P.","title":"Outlive: The Science and Art of Longevity","year":2023},{"author":"Olshansky, S.J.","title":"From Lifespan to Healthspan (JAMA)","year":2018}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"What does compression of morbidity mean?","choices":["Living as long as possible at any cost","Squeezing the sick, declining phase into the shortest window at the end of life","Avoiding all illness forever"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fries'' 1980 insight: the win is not more total years, it is fewer years spent in decline — dying at 88 after hiking at 80, not after 20 years of frailty."},{"q":"Why must the reserves for your marginal decade be built decades earlier?","choices":["Because gyms have age limits","Because VO2max and muscle decline on a compounding slope you cannot reverse from zero at 80","Because insurance requires it"],"correct":1,"explain":"Capability falls roughly 10% per decade after 30. You arrive at your last decade with whatever reserve you built — the climb cannot start there."}]'::jsonb,
 200, 1, 'foundations'),

-- ─── Lesson 2 · Foundations ───
('longevity', 'hierarchy-of-longevity-levers',
 'The Hierarchy of Levers',
 'What actually moves the needle — ranked by mortality data, not marketing budgets',
 'Longevity content is 90% noise about the last 1% of gains. Here is the honest hierarchy: the five levers with massive human mortality data, and why everything else is a rounding error until these are handled.',
 'strong', 6,
 ARRAY['One ranked map for the whole course — no more guessing what matters','Protects you from spending money on the bottom of the pyramid','Each lever links to its own protocol lesson'],
 E'Open any longevity feed and you will see ice baths, NAD+ drips, continuous glucose monitors and 30-ingredient powders. Meanwhile the five levers with the largest human mortality datasets get a fraction of the airtime — because nobody profits when you go for a run.\n\nHere is the hierarchy this course follows, ranked by the size and consistency of the evidence.\n\n**Lever 1 — Cardiorespiratory fitness (VO2max)**\nIn a 2018 JAMA study of 122,007 patients, Mandsager and colleagues found low fitness carried a mortality risk comparable to or worse than smoking, diabetes or coronary disease — and the benefit kept climbing with fitness, with no upper limit found. Elite-fitness subjects had roughly *5× lower* mortality than the least fit. No drug, food or supplement comes close. Lesson 3 is the protocol.\n\n**Lever 2 — Strength and muscle mass**\nGrip strength predicted death in 139,691 people across 17 countries (PURE study) — more strongly than systolic blood pressure. Muscle is your armour against falls, frailty and metabolic disease. Lesson 4.\n\n**Lever 3 — Sleep**\nConsistently sleeping under 6 hours associates with roughly 12% higher all-cause mortality in meta-analysis, and mid-life short sleep tracks with later dementia. Sleep is when repair happens; you cannot out-train its absence. Lesson 6.\n\n**Lever 4 — Metabolic health**\nInsulin resistance sits upstream of the biggest killers: heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes, and much of dementia risk. It is measurable, and it responds within weeks to the levers above plus food. Lessons 5 and 7.\n\n**Lever 5 — Connection and purpose**\nThe least marketable lever with shockingly hard data: social isolation carries mortality risk in the same class as light smoking (Holt-Lunstad meta-analysis, 308,849 people). Lesson 8.\n\n**How big is the whole pyramid?**\nA 2018 Circulation study following 123,000+ Americans found that five low-tech factors — not smoking, healthy weight, regular activity, decent diet, moderate alcohol — predicted **12–14 extra years** of life expectancy at age 50. That is the size of the prize sitting in the basics.\n\n**What the evidence does NOT support**\nNo supplement, biohack or device has mortality evidence within an order of magnitude of any lever above. Optimising your NAD+ while sleeping 5 hours is rearranging deck chairs. Lesson 9 walks the supplement graveyard by name.\n\n**The rule for the rest of this course**\nBefore adopting anything new, ask: *which lever does this pull, and how hard?* If the answer is "none of the five", it is entertainment, not longevity.',
 'Attention is your scarcest resource. This ranking — fitness, strength, sleep, metabolic health, connection — is where decades of mortality data say to spend it, and the standard every shiny new hack must be measured against.',
 ARRAY['Five levers with massive mortality data: VO2max, strength, sleep, metabolic health, connection.','Low cardiorespiratory fitness rivals smoking as a mortality risk (Mandsager, 122k patients).','Five basic lifestyle factors predict 12–14 extra years at age 50 — the basics ARE the biohack.'],
 ARRAY['Rate yourself 1–5 on each lever — your lowest score is your highest-return training ground.','Unfollow one account that sells bottom-of-pyramid hacks; the attention belongs to your weakest lever.'],
 '[{"author":"Mandsager, K. et al.","title":"Association of Cardiorespiratory Fitness With Long-term Mortality (JAMA Network Open)","year":2018},{"author":"Leong, D.P. et al.","title":"Prognostic value of grip strength: PURE study (The Lancet)","year":2015},{"author":"Li, Y. et al.","title":"Impact of Healthy Lifestyle Factors on Life Expectancies in the US Population (Circulation)","year":2018},{"author":"Holt-Lunstad, J. et al.","title":"Social Relationships and Mortality Risk: A Meta-analytic Review (PLoS Medicine)","year":2010}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"Which lever tops the hierarchy by mortality evidence?","choices":["Supplement optimisation","Cardiorespiratory fitness (VO2max)","Cold exposure"],"correct":1,"explain":"In 122,007 patients, low fitness rivalled or exceeded smoking and diabetes as a mortality risk — and the benefit had no found upper limit."},{"q":"How much extra life expectancy did five basic lifestyle factors predict at age 50?","choices":["2–3 years","12–14 years","30 years"],"correct":1,"explain":"Li et al. (Circulation 2018): not smoking, healthy weight, activity, diet and moderate alcohol — the unsexy basics carry the bulk of the prize."},{"q":"A new gadget claims longevity benefits. What is the first question to ask?","choices":["How many followers does the founder have","Which of the five levers does it pull, and how hard","Is it expensive enough to work"],"correct":1,"explain":"If it does not meaningfully pull fitness, strength, sleep, metabolic health or connection, it is entertainment — not longevity."}]'::jsonb,
 201, 2, 'foundations'),

-- ─── Lesson 3 · Protocol ───
('longevity', 'vo2max-strongest-predictor',
 'VO2max: The Strongest Predictor',
 'The single number most tied to how long you live — and the weekly template that raises it',
 'Cardiorespiratory fitness predicts mortality better than smoking status, diabetes or blood pressure. It is also brutally trainable at any age. Here is the 80/20 weekly structure that moves it.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['Targets the number with the strongest mortality association in exercise science','Simple weekly template: a zone 2 base plus one hard interval session','Fully compatible with your existing training and check-ins'],
 E'If you could pick one number to know about a stranger and bet on their lifespan, the evidence says: pick VO2max — the maximal rate at which their body uses oxygen.\n\n**The data**\nMandsager''s 2018 study of 122,007 treadmill-tested patients: each step up in fitness meant lower mortality, with the biggest single jump between the *least* fit group and the merely *below-average* group — and no ceiling found at the top. A 2009 JAMA meta-analysis (Kodama, 102,980 people) put numbers on it: every 1-MET increase in fitness — roughly 3.5 ml/kg/min — associated with a **13% drop in all-cause mortality**. Going from unfit to fit buys risk reductions that no prescription can match.\n\n**Why it decides your marginal decade**\nEveryday life has a fixed oxygen cost. Climbing stairs costs roughly 4 METs whether you are 30 or 85. Since VO2max falls ~10% per decade after 30, the question is where your slope started. A 50-year-old with a VO2max of 40 hits 80 with room to spare; one starting at 26 hits 80 unable to climb stairs without resting. Same slope — different starting altitude.\n\n**The protocol: 80/20**\nDecades of endurance research (Seiler''s polarised-training work) converge on the same split — most volume easy, a small slice very hard:\n\n**Base — zone 2, 2–4 sessions/week (~45–60 min).** Conversational pace: you can speak in full sentences, but singing would be hard. This builds the mitochondrial engine. The Vault''s zone 2 lesson covers the mechanism — this is the same tool, aimed at lifespan.\n\n**Peak — one VO2max session/week.** The workhorse: **4×4 intervals** — 4 minutes at a pace you could *just* hold for 8, then 3 minutes easy, four times. Norwegian trials used this exact structure to lift VO2max in everyone from students to heart patients. One session a week is enough; two is a ceiling.\n\n**What the evidence does NOT support**\nThe idea that hard cardio is dangerous for ordinary trainees, or that walking alone maximises the benefit. Walking is a superb floor — but the mortality curve keeps paying *above* it, and only harder work moves VO2max meaningfully once you are past beginner gains.\n\n**Progress markers**\nNo lab needed. Watch your pace at the same easy heart rate, your 4-minute interval pace, or resting heart rate trending down. Log sessions in your check-in — the streak is the program.',
 'Every 1-MET gain in fitness associates with ~13% lower all-cause mortality — the steepest risk-for-effort trade available anywhere in health. One hard session plus an easy base per week is the whole entry fee.',
 ARRAY['VO2max is the strongest single mortality predictor in exercise science.','Every 1-MET fitness gain ≈ 13% lower all-cause mortality (Kodama meta-analysis).','Template: 2–4 zone 2 sessions + one 4×4 interval session per week.'],
 ARRAY['Schedule one 4×4 interval session this week (bike, row, run or hill walk).','Do one 45-minute zone 2 session today at true conversational pace.'],
 '[{"author":"Mandsager, K. et al.","title":"Association of Cardiorespiratory Fitness With Long-term Mortality (JAMA Network Open)","year":2018},{"author":"Kodama, S. et al.","title":"Cardiorespiratory Fitness as a Quantitative Predictor of All-Cause Mortality (JAMA)","year":2009},{"author":"Seiler, S.","title":"What is Best Practice for Training Intensity Distribution in Endurance Athletes?","year":2010},{"author":"Helgerud, J. et al.","title":"Aerobic High-Intensity Intervals Improve VO2max More Than Moderate Training (MSSE)","year":2007}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"Roughly how much lower is all-cause mortality per 1-MET gain in fitness?","choices":["2%","13%","50%"],"correct":1,"explain":"Kodama''s 102,980-person meta-analysis: ~13% per MET — which is why moving from unfit to average-fit is the single biggest health trade available."},{"q":"What is the 4×4 protocol?","choices":["4 sets of 4 reps at maximum weight","4 minutes hard (a pace you could just hold for 8), 3 minutes easy, repeated 4 times","44 minutes of sprinting"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Norwegian interval structure used in trials from students to cardiac patients — one session a week meaningfully lifts VO2max."},{"q":"Why does VO2max decide late-life independence?","choices":["It does not — only genetics matter","Daily tasks have a fixed oxygen cost, and your reserve above that cost shrinks every decade","High VO2max prevents all disease"],"correct":1,"explain":"Stairs cost ~4 METs at any age. VO2max falls ~10% per decade — so your starting altitude decides whether stairs are trivial or impossible at 80."}]'::jsonb,
 202, 3, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 4 · Protocol ───
('longevity', 'strength-longevity-organ',
 'Strength: The Longevity Organ',
 'Grip strength out-predicts blood pressure — muscle is the retirement account you draw on at 80',
 'After 50 you can lose 1–2% of muscle per year unless you fight for it. Strength predicts survival, prevents the falls that end independence, and responds to training at every age tested.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['Grounded in the largest strength-mortality datasets (PURE, 139k people)','Minimum effective dose: 2 sessions/week, six movement patterns','Directly protects against falls — the event that ends most independent lives'],
 E'In the PURE study, researchers followed 139,691 adults across 17 countries with a device that measures grip strength. The result: every 5 kg drop in grip associated with a 16% rise in all-cause mortality — making grip a *stronger* mortality predictor than systolic blood pressure. Nobody dies of weak hands. Grip is a window into total-body muscle, and muscle is a longevity organ.\n\n**What muscle does besides move you**\n- **Glucose sink.** Muscle clears the majority of post-meal glucose. More muscle = better insulin sensitivity = Lever 4 handled at the root.\n- **Protein reserve.** In illness or injury, your body cannibalises muscle to fuel immunity and repair. Survivors of major illness are disproportionately the people who walked in strong.\n- **Fall insurance.** After 65, falls are a leading trigger of the dependence spiral: fracture, hospital, deconditioning. Strength — especially leg and hip power — is what catches a stumble before it becomes a fall.\n\n**Sarcopenia is a choice (mostly)**\nMuscle loss of 1–2% per year after 50 is the *untrained* trajectory. Trained 70-year-olds routinely out-muscle sedentary 40-year-olds, and resistance-training trials in 80- and 90-year-olds still produce meaningful strength gains. The adaptation machinery never fully closes — but the cost of rebuilding rises every decade you wait.\n\n**The protocol: 2×/week, six patterns**\nA 2022 meta-analysis (Momma, British Journal of Sports Medicine) found 30–60 minutes of muscle-strengthening work per week associated with 10–17% lower all-cause mortality. The template:\n\n- **Squat** pattern (goblet squat, leg press) — stairs and chairs at 85\n- **Hinge** (deadlift, hip thrust) — picking things up safely forever\n- **Push** (press-up, overhead press)\n- **Pull** (row, pull-down) — posture and grip\n- **Carry** (farmer''s walk) — luggage, groceries, grandchildren\n- **Balance element** (single-leg work) — the fall-proofing multiplier\n\nTwo full-body sessions, 2–4 hard sets per pattern per week, weights you could lift 2–3 more times when the set ends. That is the whole entry fee.\n\n**What the evidence does NOT support**\nThat lifting is dangerous for older adults (supervised programs show the opposite — frailty is the danger), or that endless light toning circuits build the reserve. Muscle answers to *progressive* load: the weight must creep up.\n\nEvery session is a deposit in the account your 80-year-old self will live off. The check-in streak keeps the deposits coming.',
 'Strength is the difference between a stumble and a hip fracture, between carrying your own life at 80 and asking permission. It predicts survival better than blood pressure — and two sessions a week maintains it.',
 ARRAY['Every 5 kg drop in grip strength ≈ 16% higher all-cause mortality (PURE, 139k people).','Muscle is a glucose sink, illness reserve and fall insurance — not just movement.','Dose: 2 full-body sessions/week across squat, hinge, push, pull, carry, balance.'],
 ARRAY['Count your week: do you hit all six patterns? Add the missing one to your next session.','Test yourself: rise from a chair 5 times without using your hands, timed — a validated frailty screen worth beating for life.'],
 '[{"author":"Leong, D.P. et al.","title":"Prognostic value of grip strength: findings from the PURE study (The Lancet)","year":2015},{"author":"Momma, H. et al.","title":"Muscle-strengthening activities and risk of all-cause mortality: meta-analysis (BJSM)","year":2022},{"author":"Srikanthan, P. & Karlamangla, A.","title":"Muscle Mass Index as a Predictor of Longevity in Older Adults (Am J Med)","year":2014},{"author":"Fiatarone, M. et al.","title":"High-intensity strength training in nonagenarians (JAMA)","year":1990}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"What did the PURE study find about grip strength?","choices":["It only matters for athletes","Each 5 kg decrease associated with 16% higher all-cause mortality — out-predicting blood pressure","It cannot be trained after 60"],"correct":1,"explain":"Grip is a proxy for total-body muscle, and muscle is a survival organ: glucose sink, illness reserve, fall insurance."},{"q":"What weekly dose of strength work associated with 10–17% lower mortality?","choices":["Daily 2-hour sessions","30–60 minutes per week","Strength training showed no association"],"correct":1,"explain":"Momma''s 2022 BJSM meta-analysis — two focused full-body sessions cover it. The dose is smaller than most people fear."},{"q":"Is meaningful strength gain still possible at 80+?","choices":["No, the window closes at 60","Yes — trials in nonagenarians produced significant gains","Only with hormone therapy"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fiatarone''s JAMA trial trained 90-year-olds with supervised progressive resistance and got large strength improvements. The machinery stays open — start where you are."}]'::jsonb,
 203, 4, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 5 · Protocol ───
('longevity', 'protein-aging-athlete',
 'Protein and the Aging Athlete',
 'Anabolic resistance means yesterday''s intake stops working — here is the dose that keeps muscle',
 'The muscle you fought for in Lesson 4 is built from dietary protein — and as you age, your body responds less to each gram. The fix is a higher, evenly spread dose. The kidney fear, for healthy people, is not supported.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['Concrete daily target in g/kg you can apply today','Explains anabolic resistance — why older muscles need bigger doses','Debunks the kidney-damage myth for healthy kidneys, with citations'],
 E'Lesson 4 made the case for muscle as a longevity organ. Muscle is built and kept from one raw material: dietary protein. And here age changes the rules.\n\n**Anabolic resistance**\nA young lifter''s muscle fires up protein synthesis on ~20 g of protein. An older adult''s muscle responds sluggishly to the same dose — researchers call it *anabolic resistance*. Moore and colleagues quantified it: older muscle needs roughly **0.40 g/kg per meal** to maximise the building response, versus ~0.24 g/kg when young. The practical translation: the older you get, the *more* protein per meal it takes to send the same "keep the muscle" signal.\n\n**The daily target**\nFor training adults who intend to keep muscle across decades, the evidence supports **1.6–2.2 g of protein per kg of bodyweight per day** — roughly double the RDA. The RDA (0.8 g/kg) was set to prevent deficiency in sedentary people, not to preserve a longevity organ under training. Morton''s 2018 meta-analysis of 49 trials found benefits of supplementation for lean mass plateauing around 1.6 g/kg — with a margin above it costing nothing but food budget.\n\nFor an 80 kg adult that is 130–175 g per day. It will not happen by accident.\n\n**Spread it — the leucine threshold**\nMuscle protein synthesis is triggered per meal, not per day. Three to four meals of 30–50 g each beats the common pattern of 10 g at breakfast and 80 g at dinner, because low-protein meals — especially with anabolic resistance — never cross the trigger threshold at all. Anchor each meal on a protein source first; the Vault''s nutrition course has the recipes.\n\n**What the evidence does NOT support**\nThe claim that high protein damages *healthy* kidneys. A 2018 meta-analysis (Devries, Journal of Nutrition) pooled randomised trials of higher- vs lower-protein diets and found no harmful effect on kidney function in people with healthy kidneys. The caution is real for *existing* kidney disease — that is a doctor conversation — but for healthy trainees the fear is folklore.\n\nAlso unsupported: that plant proteins cannot do the job. They can — plant-forward eaters just need slightly larger portions and variety (soy, legumes, grains) to match the leucine content.\n\n**The check-in connection**\nProtein is the quietest lever in this course: no session required, just architecture. Set the target, anchor every meal, and the strength work from Lesson 4 has its raw material for the next forty years.',
 'You can train perfectly and still lose the muscle war at the dinner table. Anabolic resistance quietly raises the price of muscle every decade — 1.6–2.2 g/kg, spread across the day, keeps paying it.',
 ARRAY['Target 1.6–2.2 g protein per kg bodyweight daily when training for the long game.','Aging muscle needs ~0.40 g/kg PER MEAL to trigger building — spread intake over 3–4 real doses.','High protein does not harm healthy kidneys (Devries meta-analysis) — the fear is folklore for healthy trainees.'],
 ARRAY['Calculate your daily target now: bodyweight in kg × 1.6 and × 2.2 — that is your range.','Audit today''s breakfast: if it is under 30 g protein, fix tomorrow''s first.'],
 '[{"author":"Morton, R.W. et al.","title":"Protein supplementation and resistance training: systematic review and meta-analysis (BJSM)","year":2018},{"author":"Moore, D.R. et al.","title":"Protein ingestion to stimulate myofibrillar protein synthesis requires greater relative protein intakes in older versus younger men (J Gerontol)","year":2015},{"author":"Devries, M.C. et al.","title":"Changes in Kidney Function Do Not Differ between Healthy Adults Consuming Higher- Compared with Lower-Protein Diets (J Nutr)","year":2018}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"What is anabolic resistance?","choices":["Refusing to take steroids","Aging muscle responding less to each gram of protein, requiring bigger per-meal doses","An allergy to protein powder"],"correct":1,"explain":"Older muscle needs roughly 0.40 g/kg per meal to trigger building vs ~0.24 g/kg when young — the same intake quietly stops working with age."},{"q":"What daily protein range does the evidence support for training adults playing the long game?","choices":["0.8 g/kg (the RDA)","1.6–2.2 g/kg","4+ g/kg"],"correct":1,"explain":"Morton''s 49-trial meta-analysis found lean-mass benefits plateauing near 1.6 g/kg; the RDA was set to prevent deficiency, not preserve a longevity organ."},{"q":"What does the evidence say about high protein and HEALTHY kidneys?","choices":["It reliably damages them","Randomised trials show no harm to healthy kidney function","Nobody has studied it"],"correct":1,"explain":"Devries'' 2018 meta-analysis of controlled trials: no harmful effect in healthy adults. Existing kidney disease is a different, doctor-level conversation."}]'::jsonb,
 204, 5, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 6 · Protocol ───
('longevity', 'sleep-repair-budget',
 'Sleep: The Nightly Repair Budget',
 'Under 6 hours shows up in mortality data — and mid-life sleep debt tracks with late-life dementia',
 'Sleep is when the repair you trained for actually happens. Short sleep associates with higher mortality and later dementia risk; the brain-cleaning mechanism is promising science. The protocol costs nothing.',
 'promising', 5,
 ARRAY['Connects sleep to the two things athletes care about: recovery now, cognition later','Honest tiering: which sleep claims are solid and which are still promising','Three-lever protocol that requires no products'],
 E'You do not adapt during training. You adapt during the repair window afterwards — and the deepest repair window is sleep. For a course about the long game, sleep earns its lever status twice: once for what it does this week, once for what the data suggests it does to your brain across decades.\n\n**The mortality data (solid)**\nCappuccio''s meta-analysis pooling over a million people: habitual sleep under 6 hours associated with roughly **12% higher all-cause mortality**. Not dramatic — but it applies to a third of adults, every night, for decades. (Curiously, very *long* sleep associates with higher mortality too, likely because illness causes long sleep rather than the reverse — association is not causation, and we tier honestly here.)\n\n**The dementia signal (promising)**\nSabia''s 2021 study in Nature Communications followed 7,959 British civil servants for 25 years: those sleeping ≤6 hours at age 50–70 had about **30% higher dementia incidence** than 7-hour sleepers, independent of the usual confounders. One study, observational — but 25 years of follow-up makes it hard to dismiss.\n\n**The mechanism (promising, and why this lesson wears that chip)**\nIn 2013, Xie and colleagues showed in mice that during sleep the brain''s **glymphatic system** opens: the space between brain cells expands and cerebrospinal fluid flushes metabolic waste — including beta-amyloid, the protein that accumulates in Alzheimer''s — at dramatically higher rates than when awake. It is elegant, it fits the human data, and human confirmation is still maturing. That is a *promising* mechanism, not settled fact, and we say so.\n\n**What the evidence does NOT support**\nThat you can bank sleep in advance, that everyone truly thrives on 5 hours (short-sleeper mutations exist but are rare), or that supplements meaningfully replace sleep architecture. Alcohol "helping you sleep" is also backwards: it fragments the second half of the night and suppresses REM.\n\n**The protocol — three levers, zero products**\n1. **A fixed wake time**, 7 days a week. The circadian anchor everything else hangs on — the Recovery course covers the light mechanics.\n2. **A 7.5–8.5 hour opportunity window.** You cannot control sleep, only the window you give it. Count backwards from your wake time and defend the boundary like a training session.\n3. **A caffeine curfew ~8 hours before bed.** Caffeine''s half-life quietly steals deep sleep even when you fall asleep fine.\n\nTrack it like training: your evening reflection and check-in already have the slot. The bar is not perfection — it is protecting the budget most nights, for decades.',
 'Every other lever in this course — training adaptation, muscle building, metabolic health, even willpower — runs its repair cycle during sleep. Protecting a 7–8 hour window is the cheapest longevity intervention that exists.',
 ARRAY['Under 6 hours of habitual sleep ≈ 12% higher all-cause mortality (million-person meta-analysis).','Mid-life short sleep tracked with ~30% higher dementia incidence over 25 years (Sabia).','Protocol: fixed wake time + 7.5–8.5 h opportunity window + caffeine curfew 8 h before bed.'],
 ARRAY['Set tomorrow''s wake time now, and count 8 hours back — that is tonight''s lights-out boundary.','Check the clock at your last caffeine today: inside 8 hours of bedtime, move it earlier tomorrow.'],
 '[{"author":"Cappuccio, F.P. et al.","title":"Sleep Duration and All-Cause Mortality: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis (Sleep)","year":2010},{"author":"Sabia, S. et al.","title":"Association of sleep duration in middle and old age with incidence of dementia (Nature Communications)","year":2021},{"author":"Xie, L. et al.","title":"Sleep Drives Metabolite Clearance from the Adult Brain (Science)","year":2013}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"Why does this lesson carry a PROMISING chip instead of STRONG?","choices":["Sleep research is all pseudoscience","The mortality association is solid, but the glymphatic brain-cleaning mechanism is still maturing in humans","The author was unsure"],"correct":1,"explain":"Honest tiering: million-person mortality data is solid; the beta-amyloid clearance mechanism is elegant mouse science with human confirmation still in progress."},{"q":"What did the 25-year Whitehall follow-up find about mid-life sleep?","choices":["Sleep has no long-term effects","≤6 hours at age 50–70 associated with ~30% higher dementia incidence","More sleep always means less dementia"],"correct":1,"explain":"Sabia 2021: persistent short sleep in mid-life tracked with meaningfully higher dementia rates decades later, independent of usual confounders."},{"q":"Which is the correct first lever of the sleep protocol?","choices":["A supplement stack","A fixed wake time, 7 days a week","Sleeping in at weekends to repay debt"],"correct":1,"explain":"The circadian system anchors on consistent wake time and morning light — weekend catch-up shifts the anchor and fragments the system further."}]'::jsonb,
 205, 6, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 7 · Protocol ───
('longevity', 'metabolic-health-waistline',
 'Metabolic Health and the Waistline',
 'Insulin resistance is upstream of the biggest killers — and it answers to muscle, movement and meals',
 'Most heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes and a slice of dementia share one upstream driver: insulin resistance. You can screen it with a tape measure and move it within weeks using tools you already have.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['One upstream target instead of four separate disease fears','Tape-measure screen you can do at home today','The 10-minute post-meal walk — the highest return-per-minute tool in the course'],
 E'Heart attack, stroke, type 2 diabetes, much of chronic kidney disease and a meaningful slice of dementia risk look like separate fates. Metabolically, they share an upstream driver: **insulin resistance** — your cells gradually going deaf to the hormone that manages fuel.\n\nThe sequence runs quietly for a decade or more: cells respond less → the pancreas shouts louder (higher insulin) → fat accumulates viscerally, around the organs → inflammation and blood pressure creep → glucose finally rises → diagnosis. By the time a fasting glucose test flags it, the process is old. The good news: every stage before diagnosis is reversible with the levers you already own.\n\n**The tape-measure screen**\nVisceral fat — the metabolically active fat around your organs — is the visible face of insulin resistance, and your waist tracks it. A 2012 meta-analysis (Ashwell) found **waist-to-height ratio** beats BMI as a screen for cardiometabolic risk. The rule is beautifully simple: **keep your waist under half your height.** 180 cm tall → waist under 90 cm, measured at the navel, relaxed. No lab, no scale debates — muscle never inflates the number the way it corrupts BMI.\n\n**The protocol — three tools you already have**\n\n**1. Muscle (Lesson 4).** Muscle clears most post-meal glucose. Every strength session builds the sink; low muscle is itself a risk state.\n\n**2. Zone 2 (Lesson 3).** Easy aerobic work multiplies mitochondria — literally more machinery for burning fuel. Zone 2 is the single best-documented exercise tool for insulin sensitivity, which is why it appears twice in this course.\n\n**3. The post-meal walk.** After eating, glucose floods in. Muscles that are *contracting* pull glucose from blood without needing much insulin at all. DiPietro''s trial in Diabetes Care: three 15-minute post-meal walks controlled 24-hour glucose in older adults at risk — with the after-dinner walk the most effective. Even 10 minutes blunts the spike. Return-per-minute, nothing in this course beats it.\n\n**What the evidence does NOT support**\nThat sugar is uniquely toxic and everything else is safe (chronic caloric surplus drives visceral fat regardless of source — though liquid sugar makes surplus effortless). That continuous glucose monitors are necessary for healthy people — a tape measure and the habits above carry most of the value at none of the cost. And "detoxes" of any kind: your liver is not waiting for a juice cleanse.\n\n**Track it**\nMeasure waist monthly, same conditions. It moves slowly — but it moves, and the direction is the truth about whether the system upstream of four major diseases is improving.',
 'Insulin resistance runs silently for a decade before any diagnosis, driving the four biggest causes of lost healthspan. A tape measure screens it; muscle, zone 2 and post-meal walks reverse it.',
 ARRAY['One upstream driver — insulin resistance — feeds heart disease, stroke, diabetes and some dementia risk.','Screen: waist under half your height (waist-to-height beats BMI — Ashwell meta-analysis).','Post-meal walks pull glucose into muscle without insulin — 10–15 min, best after dinner.'],
 ARRAY['Measure your waist at the navel today and divide by your height — over 0.5 is your signal.','Walk 10 minutes after your largest meal today; feel how little it costs.'],
 '[{"author":"Ashwell, M. et al.","title":"Waist-to-height ratio is a better screening tool than waist circumference and BMI for cardiometabolic risk (Obesity Reviews)","year":2012},{"author":"DiPietro, L. et al.","title":"Three 15-min Bouts of Moderate Postmeal Walking Significantly Improves 24-h Glycemic Control (Diabetes Care)","year":2013},{"author":"Reaven, G.","title":"Role of Insulin Resistance in Human Disease (Diabetes)","year":1988}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"What is the tape-measure rule for metabolic screening?","choices":["Waist under half your height","BMI under 25","Wrist size equals ankle size"],"correct":0,"explain":"Waist-to-height ratio out-screens BMI for cardiometabolic risk and is immune to the muscular-person BMI problem."},{"q":"Why do post-meal walks work so well?","choices":["They burn hundreds of calories","Contracting muscle pulls glucose from blood with little insulin needed","They speed up stomach emptying"],"correct":1,"explain":"Active muscle takes up glucose through a separate, insulin-light pathway — walking during the post-meal flood blunts the spike at almost zero cost."},{"q":"Which claim does the evidence NOT support?","choices":["Muscle improves insulin sensitivity","Healthy people need continuous glucose monitors to manage metabolic health","Zone 2 builds mitochondria"],"correct":1,"explain":"For healthy people, the tape measure plus muscle, zone 2 and post-meal movement carry most of the value — the gadget is optional, the habits are not."}]'::jsonb,
 206, 7, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 8 · Protocol ───
('longevity', 'connection-purpose-longevity',
 'Connection and Purpose: The Soft Lever With Hard Data',
 'Isolation carries mortality risk in smoking''s class — and your tribe is a longevity protocol',
 'The least marketable longevity lever has some of the hardest data: social connection and a reason to get up in the morning both predict survival at magnitudes rivalling classic risk factors.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['Reframes community from nice-to-have to measured mortality lever','Purpose data: measurable protection, not motivational poster material','Concrete weekly protocol — connection is trainable like any other capacity'],
 E'If a pill reduced all-cause mortality as much as strong social ties do, it would be the best-selling drug in history. Instead this lever is free, unmarketable — and mostly ignored by the optimisation crowd busy titrating supplements.\n\n**The isolation data**\nHolt-Lunstad''s meta-analysis pooled 148 studies and 308,849 participants: people with stronger social relationships had **~50% higher odds of survival** over the study periods. The effect size sits in the same class as quitting smoking and exceeds the effects of obesity and physical inactivity in the same framework. A follow-up meta-analysis found loneliness and living alone each associated with roughly 26–32% higher mortality. This is not sentiment; it is one of the most replicated findings in health psychology.\n\nMechanisms are mundane and powerful: connected people have buffered stress physiology, someone who notices when something is off, higher adherence to everything (training included), and more reasons to stay capable.\n\n**The purpose data**\nBoyle followed 1,238 older adults: those reporting high *purpose in life* had roughly **half the mortality rate** over five years of those without, controlling for depression and disease. Hill and Turiano replicated the direction across 6,163 adults over 14 years — purpose predicted survival at every age, not just in retirement. The Japanese concept *ikigai* — a reason to get up in the morning — names the same finding; the famous Okinawan longevity stories are observational and confounded, but the prospective purpose studies stand on their own.\n\n**Honest tiering inside the lever**\nThese are observational associations — you cannot randomise people into loneliness for a decade. But the dose-response patterns, replication count and plausible mechanisms make this as strong as observational evidence gets. What the evidence does NOT support: that online parasocial connection substitutes for the real thing, or that purpose must be grand. "my grandchildren", "my crew", "the thing I am building" all count — the measurement is whether your days feel *directed*.\n\n**The protocol — train it like a capacity**\n1. **A standing weekly anchor.** One recurring commitment where people expect you: a training crew, a Sunday call, a club. Scheduled beats spontaneous — spontaneous decays.\n2. **Train with others when you can.** It double-counts: every session pulls Lever 1 or 2 *and* this one. Your tribe in this app exists precisely for that — a shared streak is a modern accountability circle.\n3. **Name your purpose in one sentence.** The Coach''s long-term goal exists for this. Review it weekly; purposeless training eventually loses to friction.\n\nThe strongest version of this course is not done alone. That is not a slogan — it is what the mortality tables say.',
 'Isolation predicts death at magnitudes rivalling smoking, and purpose halves mortality rates in prospective cohorts. Your tribe and your stated goal are not app features — they are longevity protocols.',
 ARRAY['Stronger social ties ≈ 50% higher survival odds across 308,849 people (Holt-Lunstad).','High purpose in life ≈ roughly half the mortality rate over 5 years (Boyle).','Protocol: one standing weekly anchor + train with others + purpose in one sentence, reviewed weekly.'],
 ARRAY['Put one recurring social-training anchor in your calendar this week — crew session, run club, standing call.','Write your one-sentence reason to stay capable for the next 30 years; store it as your long-term goal in the Coach.'],
 '[{"author":"Holt-Lunstad, J. et al.","title":"Social Relationships and Mortality Risk: A Meta-analytic Review (PLoS Medicine)","year":2010},{"author":"Holt-Lunstad, J. et al.","title":"Loneliness and Social Isolation as Risk Factors for Mortality (Perspect Psychol Sci)","year":2015},{"author":"Boyle, P.A. et al.","title":"Purpose in Life Is Associated With Mortality Among Community-Dwelling Older Persons (Psychosom Med)","year":2009},{"author":"Hill, P.L. & Turiano, N.A.","title":"Purpose in Life as a Predictor of Mortality Across Adulthood (Psychological Science)","year":2014}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"How large was the survival effect of strong social relationships in the Holt-Lunstad meta-analysis?","choices":["Negligible","~50% higher odds of survival — in the same class as quitting smoking","Only relevant for the elderly"],"correct":1,"explain":"148 studies, 308,849 people: relationship strength predicted survival at magnitudes that rival classic medical risk factors."},{"q":"What did Boyle''s purpose study find?","choices":["Purpose is unmeasurable","High purpose in life associated with roughly half the mortality rate over 5 years","Purpose only matters if it is career-related"],"correct":1,"explain":"Controlling for depression and disease, older adults with a clear sense of purpose died at about half the rate — and Hill & Turiano replicated the direction across adulthood."},{"q":"Why does training with your tribe double-count?","choices":["You burn twice the calories","Each session pulls a physical lever AND the connection lever simultaneously","It does not — training should be solitary"],"correct":1,"explain":"A shared session is fitness or strength work plus a standing social anchor — two mortality levers in one time slot."}]'::jsonb,
 207, 8, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 9 · Protocol ───
('longevity', 'supplement-graveyard',
 'The Supplement Graveyard',
 'Resveratrol, NAD+ boosters, antioxidant megadoses — what the outcome data actually says',
 'The longevity supplement industry sells mechanisms, not outcomes. Here lies what the human data buried, the two modest exceptions worth knowing, and the filter that protects your money and attention.',
 'speculative', 6,
 ARRAY['Named claims checked against outcome data, not mechanism hype','Two honest exceptions where evidence is real but modest','A reusable filter for every future longevity product'],
 E'Every supplement in this graveyard once had a beautiful mechanism story, a charismatic scientist and a market. What none of them have is human outcome data that survives contact with the levers in Lessons 3–8. This lesson wears the *speculative* chip deliberately — because speculation is the industry''s product, and naming that honestly is the lesson.\n\n**Resveratrol — the red wine molecule**\nThe 2000s story: activate sirtuins, live like a calorie-restricted mouse. The human reality: the InCHIANTI cohort measured actual resveratrol metabolites in 783 older adults over 9 years — **no association** with mortality, cardiovascular disease, cancer or inflammation markers. GSK bought the flagship sirtuin company for $720 million and later shelved the program. The mice were interesting. You are not a mouse.\n\n**NAD+ boosters (NR, NMN)**\nTrue: NAD+ declines with age, and boosters raise blood NAD+ levels. Also true: raising a biomarker is not an outcome. Human trials to date show changed blood chemistry and little else clinically meaningful — no trial shows NAD+ boosters extend human healthspan or lifespan. Perhaps future trials will. Today you would be paying a premium to change a lab number.\n\n**Antioxidant megadoses — the cautionary headstone**\nThe strongest grave in the yard, because here the data went *negative*. The Cochrane meta-analysis (Bjelakovic, 78 trials, ~297,000 people): beta-carotene and vitamin E supplementation associated with slightly **increased** all-cause mortality. Worse for athletes: trials found high-dose vitamin C/E can *blunt training adaptations* — oxidative stress is part of the signal your training sends. Swallowing megadoses to fight it fights your own progress.\n\n**The two modest exceptions**\nHonesty cuts both ways — two compounds have real, if unglamorous, evidence:\n- **Creatine monohydrate**: the most-studied sports supplement in history; reliably supports strength and lean mass (the Lever-2 currency), with emerging but unsettled work on cognition. Cheap, safe, boring — the profile hype cannot fake. Buy the plain monohydrate; every fancier form is marketing.\n- **Omega-3 / fish oil**: modest cardiovascular benefit signals in meta-analyses, strongest for people who eat little fish. Eating fish twice a week likely covers it — the food beats the capsule.\nBoth support levers; neither replaces one.\n\n**What the evidence does NOT support**\nThe entire framing that aging can currently be supplemented against. If any compound moved human mortality the way VO2max, strength, sleep, metabolic health or connection do, it would headline Lesson 2 — and this course would say so.\n\n**The filter — use it forever**\nBefore any longevity product: 1) Is there *human outcome* data, or only mechanism and biomarkers? 2) Which of the five levers does it pull, and by how much compared to training? 3) Who profits if I believe this? Mechanism + mouse + subscription is the industry''s entire formula. Your counter-formula is Lessons 3–8 — which cost almost nothing and actually move the tables.',
 'The supplement industry monetises the gap between mechanism and outcome. Knowing what the human data buried — and the two modest exceptions — protects the money and attention your real levers need.',
 ARRAY['Resveratrol: no association with mortality or disease in humans measured over 9 years (InCHIANTI).','Antioxidant megadoses: slightly INCREASED mortality in Cochrane meta-analysis and can blunt training adaptations.','Exceptions are modest: creatine (strength/lean mass) and omega-3 (modest cardio signal) — supplements support levers, never replace them.'],
 ARRAY['Audit your supplement shelf with the filter: human outcomes? which lever? who profits?','Redirect one supplement subscription''s monthly cost toward something pulling a real lever — shoes, a gym month, protein-rich food.'],
 '[{"author":"Semba, R.D. et al.","title":"Resveratrol Levels and All-Cause Mortality in Older Community-Dwelling Adults (JAMA Internal Medicine)","year":2014},{"author":"Bjelakovic, G. et al.","title":"Antioxidant supplements for prevention of mortality (Cochrane Database Syst Rev)","year":2012},{"author":"Ristow, M. et al.","title":"Antioxidants prevent health-promoting effects of physical exercise in humans (PNAS)","year":2009},{"author":"Kreider, R.B. et al.","title":"ISSN position stand: safety and efficacy of creatine supplementation (JISSN)","year":2017}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"What did the InCHIANTI study find about resveratrol in humans?","choices":["It extended lifespan by 5 years","No association with mortality, cardiovascular disease or cancer over 9 years","It worked only in women"],"correct":1,"explain":"Measured metabolites in 783 adults, 9-year follow-up: nothing. The sirtuin story was compelling in mice; the human outcome data buried it."},{"q":"Why are antioxidant megadoses the cautionary headstone of the graveyard?","choices":["They are merely useless","Cochrane found slightly increased mortality, and trials show they can blunt training adaptations","They are too expensive"],"correct":1,"explain":"Beta-carotene and vitamin E supplementation trended toward higher mortality across ~297,000 trial participants — and oxidative stress is part of the adaptation signal your training depends on."},{"q":"What is the three-question filter for any longevity product?","choices":["Price, taste, packaging","Human outcome data? Which lever does it pull vs training? Who profits if I believe?","Is it natural, organic, ancient"],"correct":1,"explain":"Mechanism + mouse data + subscription is the industry formula. Outcomes, levers and incentives are the counter-formula."}]'::jsonb,
 208, 9, 'protocol'),

-- ─── Lesson 10 · Recap ───
('longevity', 'hundred-year-operating-system',
 'Your 100-Year Operating System',
 'The whole course as one repeatable week — and the only metric that matters: showing up for decades',
 'Nine lessons collapse into one weekly template you can run for the rest of your life: three strength or interval anchors, a zone 2 base, a protein floor, a sleep window, a waist check and one social anchor.',
 'strong', 4,
 ARRAY['One-page template — the entire course in a repeatable week','Maps every element to your existing check-in habits','Built for decades: designed to survive bad weeks, not demand perfect ones'],
 E'Nine lessons, five levers, decades of mortality data — and none of it matters unless it compresses into something you can run on an ordinary Tuesday for thirty years. Here is the operating system.\n\n**The weekly template**\n\n*Training (Levers 1–2):*\n- **2× full-body strength** — six patterns: squat, hinge, push, pull, carry, balance (Lesson 4)\n- **2–4× zone 2**, 45–60 min conversational pace (Lesson 3)\n- **1× 4×4 VO2max intervals** (Lesson 3)\n\n*Daily architecture (Levers 3–4):*\n- **Protein floor**: 1.6–2.2 g/kg, spread over 3–4 meals (Lesson 5)\n- **Sleep window**: fixed wake time, 7.5–8.5 h opportunity, caffeine curfew (Lesson 6)\n- **Post-meal walk** after the largest meal (Lesson 7)\n\n*Weekly anchors (Lever 5 + measurement):*\n- **One standing social-training commitment** — your tribe session counts double (Lesson 8)\n- **Monthly**: waist-to-height check — under 0.5 (Lesson 7)\n- **Never**: money on anything that fails the graveyard filter (Lesson 9)\n\nThat is the whole system. Roughly 6–8 hours a week — less time than most people spend scrolling content *about* longevity.\n\n**Make it survive contact with life**\nA system for decades must absorb bad weeks without collapsing. Two rules:\n\n1. **The floor rule.** On overwhelmed weeks, the system shrinks instead of stopping: one strength session, one zone 2, protein at every meal, the sleep window. The floor keeps the identity alive — and identity, as the Inner Work course teaches, is what carries behaviour across years. If you have not run a WOOP on your biggest obstacle to consistency, that lesson is the perfect companion to this one.\n\n2. **Never miss twice.** One missed day is life; two is the start of a new trajectory. This is exactly what your check-in streak measures — the app''s core loop *is* the longevity loop.\n\n**Start where you are**\nIf you currently train zero times a week, do not install the full template on Monday. The Circulation data from Lesson 2 is clear: the biggest mortality gains come from the *first* steps — unfit to somewhat-fit is the largest jump on the whole curve. One strength session and two walks is a magnificent week one.\n\n**The long view**\nYou now know what most people never learn: that the last decade of your life is being negotiated *this* decade, in ordinary weeks like the coming one. The tools are unglamorous — a barbell, a conversational run, a bedtime, a protein target, a crew, a tape measure. The evidence behind them embarrasses every product ever sold against aging.\n\nRun the week. Then run it again. That is the entire secret — and it is yours now.',
 'A longevity practice only works at the timescale of decades — which means it must be simple enough to repeat forever and flexible enough to survive real life. This template is the whole course in one week.',
 ARRAY['The week: 2× strength + 2–4× zone 2 + 1× intervals + protein floor + sleep window + social anchor.','The floor rule: bad weeks shrink the system, never stop it — identity survives on the floor.','Never miss twice — your check-in streak is the longevity metric.'],
 ARRAY['Lay this week''s template into your calendar right now — anchors first, then fill.','Define your personal floor (the shrunken week you can ALWAYS run) and write it where you plan.'],
 '[{"author":"Li, Y. et al.","title":"Impact of Healthy Lifestyle Factors on Life Expectancies in the US Population (Circulation)","year":2018},{"author":"Fries, J.F.","title":"Aging, natural death, and the compression of morbidity (NEJM)","year":1980},{"author":"Attia, P.","title":"Outlive: The Science and Art of Longevity","year":2023}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q":"What is the floor rule?","choices":["Train on the floor for core strength","On overwhelmed weeks the system shrinks to a minimum version instead of stopping","Always train to failure"],"correct":1,"explain":"A decades-long system must absorb bad weeks. The floor — one strength session, one zone 2, protein, sleep window — keeps the identity and the streak alive."},{"q":"Where do the biggest mortality gains on the fitness curve come from?","choices":["Going from very fit to elite","Going from unfit to somewhat fit — the first steps","Gains are evenly spread"],"correct":1,"explain":"The steepest part of the risk curve is at the bottom: the least fit gain the most from the first consistent steps. Starting small is not a compromise — it is the highest-return move available."},{"q":"What is the single behaviour metric this course ultimately reduces to?","choices":["Your one-rep max","Showing up repeatedly for decades — the streak","Your supplement count"],"correct":1,"explain":"Every lever compounds only through repetition across years. Never miss twice; the check-in streak is the longevity loop made visible."}]'::jsonb,
 209, 10, 'recap')

